<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spahis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1834–1962</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spahis were light cavalry regiments of the French Army recruited mainly among the Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> populations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, first organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 1834. Their name came from the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">sipahi</a> cavalry. Spahi units served in French colonial campaigns, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> and in both world wars, and were also raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spahis.jpg" alt="Spahis" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1st_Spahis_standard_guard_Bastille_Day_2008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
